## Moves instead of words

<At levels="simple">

An agent acts by writing text: the model writes a tool call, and the harness carries it out
(<ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />). A game player or a robot needs moves, not sentences. Could a model predict the moves
directly?

The classic way to learn moves is <GlossaryPopover term="reinforcement-learning">reinforcement learning</GlossaryPopover>: try things, collect
rewards, and do more of what earned them.<Cite id="sutton2018" /> This chapter shows another way, a <GlossaryPopover term="decision-transformer">Decision Transformer</GlossaryPopover>.
Record lots of games, write each one down as tokens, and
train a transformer to predict the next move the way GPT-2 predicts the next word (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />).<Cite id="chen2021decision" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In <ChapterRef to="the-agent-loop" />, a language model acts by writing text: it describes a tool call, and the harness
carries it out. A game player or a robot arm needs moves, many of them, not paragraphs. Could a
model predict the moves themselves?

The classic answer is <GlossaryPopover term="reinforcement-learning">reinforcement learning</GlossaryPopover>: a player tries moves, collects
rewards, and does more of what earned them, by trial and error.<Cite id="sutton2018" /> A <GlossaryPopover term="decision-transformer">Decision Transformer</GlossaryPopover>
takes another route, and never plays while it learns.
Record many runs of the game by any players, good or bad, and write each run down as a sequence of
tokens. Then train a transformer to predict the next move, the same way GPT-2 learned to predict the
next word (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />, <ChapterRef to="generating-text" bare />).<Cite id="chen2021decision" />

</At>

## The game

<At levels="simple">

The game is a 5 by 5 grid. The player starts in the bottom-left corner and wants the flag in the
top-right corner. Each move costs 1 point, the flag earns 20, and stepping into one of the three
pits costs 10 more and ends the game. After 16 moves the game ends anyway. The best score is 12:
the shortest route is 8 moves, and 20 − 8 = 12.

The model learns from 4,000 recorded games. Some players moved at random; others headed for the
flag but often slipped. Most games went badly, and only 413, about one in ten, scored 12.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The game is a 5 by 5 grid. The player starts in the bottom-left corner, A1, and wants the flag in
the top-right corner, E5. Each move costs 1 point, reaching the flag earns 20, and three cells are
pits: stepping into one costs 10 more and ends the run. After 16 moves the run ends anyway. The
best possible score is 12: the shortest route takes 8 moves, and 20 − 8 = 12.

The training data is 4,000 recorded runs. A third of them (1,365) come from a player that moves at
random. The rest (2,635) come from a scripted player that heads for the flag but, on every move, has
a chance of moving at random instead, set anywhere from 10% to 70% for each run. Most runs went
badly: 2,272 fell into a pit, and 368 ran out of moves. Of the 1,360 that reached the flag, only
413, about one run in ten overall, did it in the fewest moves and scored 12.

</At>

## A game as tokens

<At levels="simple">

Each step of a game becomes three tokens: the score still to earn, the cell the player is in, and
the move it made. A game that scored 12 starts with the tokens 12, A1, →. The model reads the tokens
so far and predicts the next move.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Each step of a run becomes three tokens: the score still to earn from here on, the cell the player
is in, and the move it made. A run that scored 12 starts with the tokens 12, A1, →. GPT-2 reads
text tokens and predicts the next one; this model reads these tokens and predicts the next move. It
is a tiny transformer with the same masked attention as GPT-2 (<ChapterRef to="attention" />), so each token sees only the
ones before it.

</At>

## Ask for a score

Here is the trained model. Choose a score to aim for: the model gets it as its first token, then
plays one move at a time.

<DecisionGrid />

<At levels="simple">

Aim for 12 and it takes a shortest route to the flag. Aim for 8 and it still gets there, but takes
the long way and scores exactly 8. Aim for −14 and it walks into a pit on its fourth move. The model
doesn't try to win: it copies the games that scored what you asked for.

For scores no game reached, it has nothing to copy, and its play is odd. Ask for more than 12 and it
plays the same 8 moves as for 12: it can't beat the best game it has seen.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Aim for 12 and it takes a shortest route, 8 moves to the flag. Aim for 8 and it still reaches the
flag, but in 12 moves, scoring exactly 8: it wanders on purpose, because runs that scored 8 did.
Every target from 4 to 12 comes out exact. Aim low, at −14, and it walks into a pit on its fourth
move, scoring −14. The model doesn't want to win. It plays like the runs that scored what you asked
for.

Two kinds of target fall outside its data. No training run scored from −11 to 3, so for those the
model has nothing to copy: from −10 to −2 it wanders until its 16 moves run out (−16), and from −1
to 3 it reaches the flag on its very last move (4). Above 12 is impossible, and there it plays the
same 8 moves as for 12: the best it has seen, and nothing better.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Inside the toy

Each token becomes a list of 24 numbers. The score still to earn is divided by 20 and multiplied
into 24 learned numbers; cells and moves each have a row in a learned table, like the word vectors
in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />. Every token also gets the row for its step number, so the model can tell how many moves
have passed. Two transformer blocks (<ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" />), each with two attention heads, refine the
vectors, and the vector at each cell token is turned into scores for the four moves. A full 16-move
run is 48 tokens. The model has 15,772 weights, trained for 4,000 steps of 128 runs each with the
same cross-entropy as GPT-2, scored on the moves only. The browser runs the same network with the
recorded weights, and a test checks it makes the same moves as the training script.

### The score still to earn

If $r_t$ is the reward for move $t$, the token before it is what the rest of the run earns,

$$\hat R_t = r_t + r_{t+1} + \dots, \qquad \hat R_{t+1} = \hat R_t - r_t$$

so after each move the game subtracts its reward and passes on what's left.<Cite id="chen2021decision" /> Aim for
12 and every move costs 1, so the tokens go 12, 13, 14, and so on up to 19 at the eighth move. Then
the flag pays 20 − 1 = 19, and nothing is left to earn.

### Aiming high from mediocre runs

Most of the training runs were bad, yet aiming for 12 gives a perfect route. Training never said
which runs were good; the score token did. Asking for 12 picks out what the 413 runs that scored 12
did in each cell. The same token sets the limit: asking for 20 can't produce a run better than any
it saw. Many reinforcement-learning methods work differently: they estimate how much reward each
move leads to and improve by picking the best, so they can, in principle, find routes better than
any they were shown.<Cite id="sutton2018" />

</At>

## Bigger versions

<At levels="simple">

Bigger Decision Transformers learned to play video games from recorded play, and did as well as or
better than the best reinforcement-learning methods given the same recordings.<Cite id="chen2021decision" /> A model
called Gato uses the same weights to play video games, describe pictures, chat, and stack blocks
with a robot arm.<Cite id="reed2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Chen and colleagues trained Decision Transformers on recorded Atari games and simulated robots, and
they matched or beat the best reinforcement-learning methods that learn from recorded data
alone.<Cite id="chen2021decision" /> Gato went further: one transformer, with the same weights, plays Atari, captions
images, chats, and stacks blocks with a real robot arm, choosing from its context whether to output
text, button presses, or joint torques.<Cite id="reed2022" /> Underneath is the idea from this chapter: if
something can be written as tokens, a transformer can learn to predict it.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A game can be written as tokens: for each step, the score still to earn, the cell, and the move.
- A Decision Transformer learns from recorded runs to predict the next move, the way GPT-2 predicts
  the next word.
- You choose the score to aim for, and it plays like the runs that scored that. It can't beat the
  best run it has seen.
- Reinforcement learning instead learns by trial and error, from rewards.

<At levels="deep">

- After each move the score still to earn drops by that move's reward: aiming for 12, it goes 12,
  13, … 19, and the flag pays the last 19.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** how do all the pieces fit together?
